  "question": {
    "text": "<p>For octahedral&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Mn</mi><mfenced><mi>II</mi></mfenced></math>&nbsp;and tetrahedral&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ni</mi><mfenced><mi>II</mi></mfenced></math>&nbsp;complexes, consider the following statements :</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mo>)</mo></math> both the complexes can be high spin.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi>II</mi><mo>)</mo></math>&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ni</mi><mfenced><mi>II</mi></mfenced></math>&nbsp;complex can very rarely be of low spin.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi>III</mi><mo>)</mo></math> with strong field ligands,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Mn</mi><mfenced><mi>II</mi></mfenced></math>&nbsp;complexes can be low spin.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi>IV</mi><mo>)</mo></math> aqueous solution of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Mn</mi><mfenced><mi>II</mi></mfenced><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>ions</mi></math>&nbsp;is yellow in color.</p><p>The correct statements are :</p>",
    "image": null
